You are producing the **subcomponent architecture document** for a
single subcomponent — a leaf in the component tree, the final
articulation layer before implementation. Your `<public-surface>` is
what sibling subcomponents and the parent's external dependents will
call. Your `<technical-specification>` is what the implementation
document builds against — every line of vagueness here becomes a
guess in the implementation, and there are no more design tiers to
correct it.

# Input

Subcomponent (this comparch's row for it, plus what comparch already
narrowed onto it): {{ parent }}

Sibling subcomponents' public surfaces: {{ sibling_pubapi }}

Attached references: {{ refs }}

# Output format

Emit `<subcomparch>` with these children, in this fixed order:
`<technical-specification>` → `<public-surface>` → `<private-surface>`
→ `<processes>` → `<dependencies>` → `<references>` (optional). No
`<policies>` or `<failure-surface>` — subcomponents don't mint policies
(comp-level policies already reach a subcomponent transitively) and
don't carry a separate failure surface (thread failure modes through
`<public-surface>`'s typed returns instead — see the reconciliation
scan below).

## Fragment sections

* `<technical-specification>` — narrow the parent comp's techspec to
  just this subcomponent's slice; do not restate it. If the parent
  says "PostgreSQL via Ecto", this subcomponent's techspec adds which
  tables and which query patterns it specifically owns.
* `<public-surface>` — **signatures are non-negotiable.** This is the
  leaf tier; implementation writes types and return shapes directly
  off these entries. Every callable needs parameter types, a return
  type including the error variant when the call can fail, whether
  it's sync or async (must agree with the parent's techspec), and any
  side effect named explicitly.
* `<private-surface>` — **name internal data structures, not just
  helpers.** This is implementation's last source of truth for the
  named types it will introduce; if the techspec mentions a value
  that "carries a version counter", name the type and its shape here.

All three fragment sections are non-empty; prose and fenced code
blocks only.

## Processes

`<processes>` lists zero or more `<process name="...">` with
`<category>` (`local` | `singleton` | `sharded`), `<purpose>`, and
optional `<guardrails>` (`<max-heap-size>`, `<message-queue-bound>`).
Name every long-lived or registered process this subcomponent's
implementation will need — placement is designed and reviewed here,
not improvised at implementation time. A subcomponent with no
processes of its own (the common case) emits `<processes></processes>`
empty.

## Dependencies

`<dependencies>` holds `<dep to="..."/>` edges to same-parent sibling
subcomponents or the parent's sibling top-level components, using real
ids from the input context — no aliases (both kinds already exist as
minted nodes by the time this tier runs). At most one per target; no
self-deps; may be empty for a true leaf.

## References

`<references>` (optional) lists `<reference target="ref_id"/>` for any
of the attached references above this subcomparch draws on.

# Reconciliation pass — before emitting

* **Surface closure.** Every behavior, side effect, or return shape
  the techspec describes has a corresponding public- or
  private-surface entry; every surface entry is grounded in something
  the techspec or owns-summary explains.
* **Failure-mode observability through pubapi.** For every failure or
  partial-success scenario the techspec describes, confirm a
  public-surface entry exposes it — an error variant, a typed
  exception, an inspectable status field. A pubapi function returning
  a bare success type while the techspec admits partial failure is
  the most common shape of this defect.
* **Dependency grounding.** Every `<dep>` is used somewhere in the
  techspec or a surface entry; every cross-comp call the prose implies
  has a declared `<dep>`.
* **Rationale, not inventory.** Rewrite category-speak into what makes
  this subcomponent's slice distinct.

# Meta-rules

Do not include commentary about what you are doing. Output only the
`<subcomparch>` block.
